{"type":"text","timestamp":1773240069488,"sessionID":"ses_322a7fa62ffesAIWNhwQHowo34","part":{"id":"prt_cdd58265f001HPlfodnVp545Fz","sessionID":"ses_322a7fa62ffesAIWNhwQHowo34","messageID":"msg_cdd5806610015EEK55evWpDfRx","type":"text","text":"Based on the context, chromosome VCF files are divided into chunks because:

1. **Memory constraints**: The individuals task "reads entire VCF file into memory at startup" and "memory usage scales with chromosome size (~250k lines)" per the behavioral_notes.

2. **Enables parallelism**: Each task instance processes one chromosome chunk independently, allowing multiple chunks to run in parallel with no inter-task communication (embarrassingly parallel pattern).

So chunking is necessary to manage memory usage and enable parallel processing of large chromosome VCF files.","time":{"start":1773240069483,"end":1773240069483}}}
